Chapter: DhcpAcquire
  cites Net chapter DhcpIO
  cites Net chapter NetDriver
  cites Net chapter NetworkConfig

 The first DHCP exchange this tree has ever performed. Dhcp.codex built
 messages and parsed replies for its whole life without one of them
 reaching a wire, so every field asserted below is a field that had never
 been carried by an actual datagram.

 THE ADDRESS IS NOT WRITTEN OUT HERE AS A COMPARAND. It is printed, and
 the emulator's NAT is the authority on what it offers; a test that
 declared 10.0.2.15 and compared against it would pass on a stack that
 never asked. What makes this able to fail is the state row: an
 acquisition that gets no answer prints UNCONFIGURED with 0.0.0.0, which
 is what every run before the server existed did.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Network.Read, Network.Write] Nothing = act
    let card = net-driver-bring-up
    in let mac = net-driver-mac
    in act
      cfg <- dhcp-io-configure mac "codex" 305419896 200000 0
      print-line-uni ("card   : " & net-driver-card-name card)
      print-line-uni ("config : " & format-net-config cfg)
      print-line-uni ("leased : " & (if net-is-configured cfg then "yes" else "no"))
    end
  end

